> [!answer]
> This is a **type-I error (α, a false positive)**.
>
> The null hypothesis — that the new drug is **no more effective** than the standard drug — was in fact **true**, but it was **rejected**; the investigator claimed a difference that does not exist.
>
> **Possible reasons:**
> - the **significance level (α) was set too high** (e.g. 0.10 instead of 0.01), so the rejection region was large;
> - **sampling error/chance** — an unrepresentative sample happened to favour the new drug;
> - **multiple testing** without adjustment, so that one of many comparisons was 'significant' by chance;
> - **bias in design or conduct** — selection bias, lack of blinding or randomisation, confounding, measurement bias;
> - inappropriate statistical test, violated assumptions, or data dredging/'p-hacking'.
>
> **Prevention:** set a stringent α, randomise and blind, control confounding, adjust for multiple comparisons, and replicate the study.
